{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788307313,"nonce":"xxW+u9siRP5dfnQ2fm/ssTB6KnE=","title":"The mod says what is wrong but not what fixes it, and the  op answers nothing: move discoverability into the mod","message":"Session 21 moved DISCIPLINE into the mod — `advance` refuses a blind advance, halts on a casualty, halts on news. This is the successor: move DISCOVERABILITY in. Raised by Evan while reviewing the M1 launch prompt: *\"is our harness that bad that we need that much?? what's redundant there, and if it's not, why's it not part of the mod?\"*\n\nHe was right. Most of the prompt's \"here are the verbs you should use\" section was redundant, because the mod already says those things at the moment of need — and the redundant half is the evidence that the pattern WORKS.\n\n## The pattern already exists, in four places\n\n- The casualty halt's own detail: *\"`triage` has the bleed clock, the rescuers and the exact `rescue` call with both ids filled in; `advance {through_casualties:\"\u003cwhy\u003e\"}` rides past it deliberately.\"*\n- `triage`'s `act` field carries the literal call to send, plus a `why` explaining why a work-priority flip is not a substitute.\n- The unread-journal refusal names the count, the seq range, the event types, and cites the M1 run that the guard exists for.\n- `VerbArgs.NearMiss`: *\"unknown arg 'at' — did you mean 'pos'? ... the call would have used a default for 'pos' and reported success.\"*\n\n**In each case the thing that reports the problem names the thing that fixes it**, and the agent needs no prompt to find it.\n\n## Where it is missing\n\n| read that shows the problem | verb that fixes it | named? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `digest.work_coverage.ok: false`, `under: [\"Doctor\"]` | `work-cover` | **no** |\n| `digest.resources.food_rot` warning / `soonest_rot_days` falling | `temp-set` | **no** |\n| `digest.temperature.out_of_range` | `temp-set` | **no** |\n| `digest.posture.ok: false` / `flee_risk` non-empty | `posture` | **no** |\n| a level the agent should be watching over time | `trends` / `history` | **no** |\n| an alert firing repeatedly that the agent has decided about | `alert-mute` | **no** |\n\nAn agent only knows these exist because a launch prompt listed them. That is exactly the failure this project already paid for: **`rescue` was shipped, forced the job, interrupted `LayDown` — and was called ZERO times in 195 operations** during the M1 run, while the response actually attempted was a work-priority flip. The verb existed and nothing pointed at it.\n\n## And the front door is shut\n\n**Op `verbs` returns `ok` with an EMPTY `data` block.** Found on 2026-09-01 when `accept/280fb78-wake-halts.py` skipped itself twice against a bench that had `alert-mute` registered all along: its precondition sent op `verbs` and dug `data.verbs`. `rwa verbs` only works because the CLIENT sends `status` and reads `data.verbs` off that.\n\nSo an agent that asks the mod \"what can I do\" gets nothing, across a 133-verb surface. Every other route to the verb list is client-side or prose.\n\n## Scope\n\n**1. Make the `verbs` op answer.** At minimum the list `status` already carries. Consider more: a one-line purpose per verb, and its argument names — the read log added for `7382bdd` means the mod can already say what a verb READ on a call, which is most of the way to saying what it accepts. Decide whether that is derivable or needs a declaration, and note that `7382bdd` rejected a 120-verb hand-written declaration for good reasons that apply here too.\n\n**2. Name the fixing verb in the reporting read.** Add a `fix` (or similarly named) field to the rows above. Keep it a VERB NAME plus, where the call is unambiguous, the call itself — `triage.act` is the worked example and is the standard to match. Do not write prose advice; name the call.\n\n**3. Do not name a verb the caller cannot use.** `dev:*` is gated; a gated suggestion is worse than none. Check the gate before suggesting.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Op `verbs` returns the registered verb list, and `accept/280fb78-wake-halts.py`'s precondition passes against it without going through `status`.\n- `digest.work_coverage` with `ok:false` names `work-cover`; `digest.posture` with `ok:false` names `posture`; a rot or out-of-range temperature row names `temp-set`.\n- Each suggestion is checked against the verb's own gate, so a suggestion is never made to a caller who would be refused.\n- A row that is FINE carries no suggestion — the cap and the three-field budget for healthy rows are unchanged.\n- Proven by a suite that reads a suggestion out of an envelope and SENDS it, the way the orchestrator sent `triage.act` verbatim in session 21.","files":null}]}